  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Hiring an Insulation Contractor for an Attached Garage: What Questions to Ask",
    "text": "Hiring an Insulation Contractor for an Attached Garage: What Questions to Ask",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Is hiring an insulation contractor necessary for an attached garage remodel?\n\nWhile insulating an open wall might look like a straightforward weekend task, an attached garage comes with specific safety, air-barrier, and thermal requirements that make professional installation worthwhile. \n\nAttached garages share walls—and often ceilings—with conditioned rooms inside your home. Local building codes require continuous air sealing at these shared boundaries to prevent carbon monoxide, vehicle exhaust, and cold drafts from entering living quarters. A professional contractor knows how to properly seal rim joists, plate connections, and utility penetrations before placing insulation.\n\nAnother critical factor is material choice and fire protection. Garage insulation cannot simply be left exposed if it poses a flame-spread risk; it must be paired with appropriate thermal barriers such as gypsum board. When you work with a contractor who handles both insulation and drywall finishing, you ensure stud cavities, headers, and corners are packed to their verified R-value without compression or voids before walls are closed up.\n\nQuestions to Ask Before You Decide:\n- Does the contractor seal air leaks with foam or caulk before installing batts?\n- Are they familiar with local fire-separation rules for walls shared with your house?\n- Can they handle both the insulation and the drywall finishing under one contract?\n\nFor comprehensive garage remodeling, framing, and insulation across the Denver area, contact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}